<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Templar Cyprus</h1><p class="st-meta">1191 CE – 1192 CE · capital Nicosia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Brief rule of Cyprus by the Knights Templar after they bought the island from Richard the Lionheart, ended by a Cypriot revolt within a year.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Having conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-komnenos-of-cyprus">Isaac Komnenos</a> in 1191, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> had no use for governing it during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a>. He sold the island to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a>, then led by Grand Master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-de-sable">Robert de Sable</a>, for 100,000 bezants, of which 40,000 were paid in advance.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The order sent only a small force, said to number about twenty knights and some soldiers, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armand-bouchart">Armand Bouchart</a> to govern from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a>. They raised heavy taxes to pay the purchase price, and their harsh rule quickly angered the Greek population, who had already suffered under Isaac.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>At Easter 1192 the Cypriots rose and besieged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a>. The knights broke out, killing many townspeople, but realised they could not hold the island. They returned it to Richard, who sold it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy of Lusignan</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-cyprus">Kingdom of Cyprus</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="theocracy, 1191 CE – 1192 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">theocracy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.40%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1191 CE – 1192 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nicosia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Robert de Sable, Armand Bouchart</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cyprus under the Knights Templar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1192&amp;focus=templar-cyprus" aria-label="Templar Cyprus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5525 906 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Templar Cyprus lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5845 1162L5845 1162M5845 1162L5845 1162"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5845 1162h0M5845 1162h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5845 1162h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5845 1162h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nicosia. <a href="/atlas?year=1192&amp;focus=templar-cyprus">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
